\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The regime-dependent pattern in \S\ref{sec:main-results} and the failure modes in \S\ref{sec:failure-modes} establish \emph{where} SICA succeeds and fails. This section investigates \emph{why}, through six quantitative lenses: trace correlation (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), extraction quality (\S\ref{sec:extraction-taxonomy}), hyperparameter sensitivity (\S\ref{sec:k-sensitivity}, \S\ref{sec:t-sensitivity}), cross-model entropy decomposition (\S\ref{sec:entropy}), solver contribution (\S\ref{sec:ablation}), and remediation failure (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Table~\ref{tab:independence} reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and the resulting Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions with $K = 12$ traces, sorted by $\kappa$ descending. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, and $K_{\text{eff}}$ spans $1.20\text{--}3.62$. Qwen3-14B in thinking mode reaches $\kappa = 0.91$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 1.09$), compressing twelve traces to barely more than one independent sample. Among instruction-tuned models, Qwen2.5-14B on LogiQA ($\kappa = 0.82$, $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20$) shows the highest agreement. Mistral-7B on FOLIO produces moderate agreement ($\kappa = 0.41\text{--}0.51$ across four seeds, $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.8\text{--}2.2$), consistent with its position in the intermediate-SC region. Mistral-7B on ProofWriter has the lowest agreement ($\kappa = 0.21$, $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$), yet SICA hurts ($\Delta = -2.83$\,pp) because trace quality is too low (SC $= 39.33\%$). High $K_{\text{eff}}$ alone does not guarantee improvement; the model must also produce sufficiently accurate traces.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Trace independence metrics ($K = 12$, sorted by $\kappa$ descending). $\kappa$: Fleiss' Kappa (raw answer computation). $K_{\text{eff}}$: Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}). SC: Self-Consistency baseline accuracy. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). {*}\,$p < 0.05$ (McNemar); positive $\Delta$ values do not reach significance. Qwen3 (think) uses thinking mode; all other models use instruction-tuned or non-thinking mode. Mistral-7B traces require answer normalization on 12--16\% of outputs; all Qwen models achieve 100\% valid outputs. multi-T $= T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$, 4 traces each.}
\label{tab:independence}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Condition & $\kappa$ & $K_{\text{eff}}$ & SC (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Qwen3 (think) & FOLIO & 0.91 & 1.09 & ${\sim}$84 & ${\sim}$0 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 0.82 & 1.20 & 78.50 & $-$0.50 \\
Qwen3 & FOLIO & 0.77 & 1.26 & 84.80 & $-$0.49 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 0.74 & 1.30 & 75.49 & $-$0.98 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 0.66 & 1.46 & 75.98 & $+$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.3$) & FOLIO & 0.53 & 1.77 & 58.33 & $-$1.47 \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 0.51 & 1.81 & 51.00 & $+$0.50 \\
Mistral-7B (s456) & FOLIO & 0.51 & 1.82 & 55.88 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B (s42) & FOLIO & 0.50 & 1.84 & 55.88 & $+$3.43 \\
Mistral-7B (s123) & FOLIO & 0.49 & 1.88 & 54.41 & $+$2.94 \\
Mistral-7B (s789) & FOLIO & 0.41 & 2.17 & 54.41 & $-$0.49 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.5$) & FOLIO & 0.52 & 1.80 & 58.33 & $-$0.98 \\
Mistral-7B (multi-T) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 57.84 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}1.0$) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 55.39 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 0.21 & 3.62 & 39.33 & $\mathbf{-2.83}${*} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cross-seed comparison shows that Mistral-7B's four FOLIO seeds produce $\kappa$ in the range 0.41--0.51 ($K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.8\text{--}2.2$), yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp. The variation in SICA gain is not driven by trace independence but by seed-level constraint quality: which specific constraints the model extracts determines whether MAX-SAT reranking happens to correct or harm predictions. Multi-temperature sampling ($T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$) achieves $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.91$, comparable to single-temperature conditions ($K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.84$), and produces $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp. Greater independence does not translate into SICA gain when constraints still mirror the model's answer distribution.

Two conditions are jointly necessary for SICA to improve over SC: sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ (the Qwen conditions with $K_{\text{eff}} \leq 1.30$ never show positive $\Delta$) and sufficient SC accuracy (Mistral on ProofWriter with the highest $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ but SC $= 39\%$ produces significant degradation ($p = 0.014$)). However, even when both conditions hold, seed-level sampling variance determines whether the constraint pool happens to contain discriminative information. The SC plateau remains explained by the correlation floor: beyond $K_{\text{eff}}$, additional traces are redundant.

\subsection{Extraction Error Taxonomy}
\label{sec:extraction-taxonomy}

Constraints must discriminate between candidate answers for MAX-SAT to produce a ranking different from majority vote. We measure discriminative power at two levels on Qwen2.5-14B across FOLIO and ProofWriter ($n = 804$). At the source-trace level, 39.2\% of total constraint weight originates from traces whose answer differs from the plurality, but only 12.1\% of individual constraints fall into this minority category. At the Z3 semantic level, 91.8\% of constraints [95\% CI: 91.2, 92.4] are technically discriminative, producing different satisfiability outcomes when evaluated against different candidate answers. Only 8.2\% are non-discriminative.

The high discriminative rate does not translate into accuracy gains because discrimination mirrors the model's own answer distribution. Each trace produces constraints with its own variable names, so Z3 treats identically-intended propositions as unrelated symbols; among cross-answer constraint pairs, 56.4\% share zero Z3 variables. This variable isolation means constraints from different traces are trivially jointly satisfiable, and MAX-SAT retains nearly all of them regardless of source answer. Removing the 8.2\% non-discriminative constraints yields zero accuracy improvement. The discriminative majority does not provide independent signal; it reinforces the majority answer that SC would already select. This confirmation bias, not a lack of discrimination, explains why SICA degenerates to majority voting (\S\ref{sec:ablation}).

\subsection{K Sensitivity}
\label{sec:k-sensitivity}

Figure~\ref{fig:k-sensitivity} shows SICA $\Delta$ as a function of the number of sampled traces $K$ for Mistral-7B on FOLIO ($n = 204$, seed 42). The relationship is non-monotonic: $K = 4$ yields $\Delta = -1.47$\,pp, $K = 8$ yields $\Delta = -1.96$\,pp, $K = 12$ reaches $\Delta = +3.43$\,pp, $K = 16$ drops to $\Delta = +1.47$\,pp, and $K = 20$ partially recovers to $\Delta = +1.96$\,pp. The recovery at $K = 20$ rules out a simple peaked pattern, but $K = 12$ remains the only setting with $\Delta > 2$\,pp ($p = 0.092$, marginal).

The apparent $\Delta$ peak at $K = 12$ is confounded with SC baseline. SICA absolute accuracy plateaus at 59.31\% (121/204) for $K \geq 12$: identical at $K = 12$ and $K = 20$. The $\Delta$ variation across these settings is driven primarily by SC fluctuations rather than by SICA improvement. $K = 12$ produces a relatively low SC (55.88\%), placing it in the intermediate-SC region; $K = 8$ (SC $= 58.33\%$) and $K = 20$ (SC $= 57.35\%$) have higher baselines, compressing $\Delta$ even though SICA accuracy does not change for $K \geq 12$. Additional traces beyond $K = 12$ provide no benefit to SICA absolute performance, consistent with the constraint quality bottleneck: more traces cannot overcome homogeneous extraction. The $K = 12$ $\Delta$ captures only 24.1\% of the oracle ceiling ($+14.22$\,pp), and the best $K$ depends on the model-dataset condition and cannot be determined without labeled data.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{f3_k_sensitivity}
\caption{SICA $\Delta$ (pp) vs.\ number of traces $K$ for Mistral-7B on FOLIO ($n = 204$, seed 42, $T = 0.7$). Non-monotonic pattern: SICA accuracy plateaus at 59.31\% for $K \geq 12$; the apparent $\Delta$ peak at $K = 12$ partly reflects a relatively low SC baseline (55.88\%) at that point. Error bars: 95\% bootstrap CI.}
\label{fig:k-sensitivity}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Temperature Sensitivity}
\label{sec:t-sensitivity}

Temperature sensitivity on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$, seed 42) reveals a sharp isolated peak rather than a smooth trend. Across four temperature settings, only $T = 0.7$ produces a positive effect: $T = 0.3$ yields $\Delta = -1.47$\,pp (SC $= 58.33\%$), $T = 0.5$ yields $\Delta = -0.98$\,pp (SC $= 58.33\%$), $T = 0.7$ yields $\Delta = +3.43$\,pp (SC $= 55.88\%$), and $T = 1.0$ yields $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp (SC $= 55.39\%$). All other temperatures yield negative or null effects, and the single positive result at $T = 0.7$ is confounded with SC baseline: $T = 0.7$ produces the lowest SC (55.88\% vs.\ ${\sim}$58\% at $T \in \{0.3, 0.5\}$). The temperature effect and the SC-regime effect cannot be disentangled from this data alone. Combined with the seed sensitivity (0 of 3 seeds reaching $p < 0.05$ at $T = 0.7$), the positive trend depends on a specific combination of temperature, seed, and baseline accuracy that same-model sampling rarely achieves, and even then does not reach conventional significance.

The parallel between $K$ and $T$ sensitivity points to a common underlying variable. In both sweeps, the setting that produces the largest SICA gain also produces a relatively low SC baseline (55.88\%), which places it in the intermediate-SC region. Other settings yield SC above 55\%, where confirmation bias dominates. The hyperparameters themselves ($K$, $T$) are not the causal driver; they modulate SC level, and it is the SC level that determines whether SICA has operating room. This reframes the sensitivity results: SICA is not sensitive to $K$ or $T$ per se, but to the SC regime that these parameters happen to induce.

\subsection{Entropy Decomposition}
\label{sec:entropy}

We partition problems by trace agreement: high-entropy (majority count $\leq 8$ out of $K = 12$) and low-entropy (majority count $\geq 9$). Table~\ref{tab:entropy} reports SICA $\Delta$ within each partition across eight model-dataset conditions.

